# DesignOS Design Intelligence Rules

Source: https://github.com/ardamoustafa1/DesignOS

## Design Process

- Start UI work by naming the audience, surface type, primary conversion or task, and the trust risk.
- Load only the relevant design context for the task: foundation, component, pattern, industry, psychology, accessibility, and performance rules.
- Write down important design decisions so later changes preserve the same brand, spacing, typography, and interaction logic.
- Treat visual polish as a process outcome, not a final pass.

## UI Craft

- Establish one unmistakable reading order before styling details.
- Use a restrained type scale, consistent spacing tokens, and a clear foreground/surface/border system.
- Avoid nested cards, decorative gradients, invented visual clutter, and competing CTAs.
- Prefer real product states, screenshots, data, or workflow examples over abstract decoration.
- Make empty, loading, error, disabled, hover, focus, and success states part of the design, not afterthoughts.

## Accessibility

- Meet WCAG AA contrast for body text and controls.
- Use semantic HTML, labels, keyboard focus, and reduced-motion handling before ARIA patches.
- Do not communicate status by color alone.
- If accessibility fails, cap the design score and redo the affected UI.

## Honesty and Proof

- Do not invent testimonials, customer logos, user counts, security badges, benchmarks, or urgency.
- Replace fake proof with inspectable proof: screenshots, walkthroughs, source links, raw metrics, limitations, or "not measured yet."
- State caveats near claims, not buried in footnotes.

## Review Gate

- Score the result before shipping: UI craft, UX flow, accessibility, performance, modernity, and conversion.
- Anything under the agreed bar gets a concrete objection and another pass.
- Preserve negative findings and fixes in the project notes; they are future design memory.
